# Machine-readable citation metadata (CFF 1.2.0). Read by GitHub's "Cite
# this repository" button and, once archiving is enabled, by Zenodo.
#
# `version` below is locked to pyproject.toml's [project].version by
# tests/test_version.py, and `date-released` is the release date of that
# version — bump BOTH as the first step of a release, before tagging (see
# "Release procedure" in CONTRIBUTING.md). There is no software DOI yet;
# once Zenodo archiving mints the concept DOI it goes under `identifiers:`
# here and in the README (issue #284). A placeholder DOI is deliberately
# not used: an unresolvable DOI in a citation file is worse than none.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "WrinkleFE"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2025-03-24
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/elhajjar1/wrinkleFE"
url: "https://github.com/elhajjar1/wrinkleFE"
abstract: >-
  WrinkleFE is a Python finite element package for predicting strength
  and stiffness knockdown in composite laminates containing fiber waviness
  defects. It combines Budiansky-Fleck kink-band theory, LaRC04/05
  failure criteria, and a three-mechanism tension knockdown model with
  3D hexahedral finite element analysis.
keywords:
  - composites
  - fiber waviness
  - wrinkle
  - finite element
  - failure analysis
  - kink-band
  - LaRC05
authors:
  - family-names: "Elhajjar"
    given-names: "Rani"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4778-5186"
    affiliation: "University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee"
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: "Elhajjar"
        given-names: "R."
    title: "Fat-tailed failure strength distributions and manufacturing defects in advanced composites"
    journal: "Scientific Reports"
    volume: 15
    pages: "25977"
    year: 2025
    doi: "10.1038/s41598-025-06693-4"
